# BrainStrike Desktop provider credentials
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` for local development and set only the
# credentials you use. Existing launch-environment variables take precedence.
# Never commit a populated `.env` or `.env.local`.
#
# Each of the seven providers below accepts exactly two names, checked in order:
#   1. BRAINSTRIKE_<PROVIDER>_API_KEY  (app-scoped override, wins)
#   2. the vendor-standard name
# Their older aliases (BS_OPENAI_API_KEY, AI_<PROVIDER>_API_KEY,
# OPENAI_API_KEY_V1/_V2, GOOGLE_API_KEY) still parse but are deprecated:
# they never authenticate, and the app reports a rename warning.
# The local runtimes' optional embeddings bearer tokens (OLLAMA_API_KEY,
# LMSTUDIO_API_KEY and their AI_* variants) are separate live names outside
# this scheme and are NOT read from these files — set them in the launch
# environment.

# OpenAI (use either name)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic (use either name)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Google AI Studio (use either name)
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter (use either name)
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Cohere embeddings (use either name)
COHERE_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_COHERE_API_KEY=

# Voyage AI embeddings (use either name)
VOYAGE_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_VOYAGE_API_KEY=

# Amazon Bedrock — TWO auth forms, and a bearer key always wins over the
# credential chain.
#
# 1. Bedrock API key / bearer token from the AWS console (use either name):
AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=
# BRAINSTRIKE_BEDROCK_API_KEY=
#
# 2. The AWS credential chain — your local profiles, SSO, credential_process,
#    and instance roles, resolved exactly as the AWS CLI does. It activates
#    only when AWS_REGION is set, NO bearer key above is set, and AWS_PROFILE
#    or AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID is present. Run `aws sso login --profile <name>`
#    first if the profile uses SSO.
# AWS_PROFILE=
#
# Either form requires a region. AWS_REGION and AWS_PROFILE are the only
# supported NON-credential names in these files: both are configuration, not
# secrets (AWS_PROFILE merely NAMES a profile). AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID is
# deliberately NOT settable here — it can still activate the chain when it is
# already in your shell environment.
AWS_REGION=

# Tavily web search (use either name)
TAVILY_API_KEY=
# BRAINSTRIKE_TAVILY_API_KEY=
